# Why we're building Redactly

It started with a simple observation: nearly everyone around me uses ChatGPT, Claude, or similar LLMs for work tasks. And nearly all of them, without thinking about it, end up sending sensitive data — colleagues' names, email addresses, contract details, internal ticket numbers.

The problem isn't malice. It's convenience. When a tool is this useful, you reach for it — and only think about the implications afterwards.

## The wrong solution: "privacy modes"

Plenty of LLM providers have responded to this concern. "Disable memory", "opt out of training", "enterprise tier without data usage". It sounds reassuring, but it misses the point.

The crucial detail: these measures limit what is *done* with the data — not whether it is transmitted at all. Your text still travels across the network to the provider's server, gets processed there, and the model generates a reply. That's how a cloud LLM fundamentally works.

PII masking *before* sending is qualitatively different. The LLM sees `[NAME_1]` instead of "John Doe". It can draw no inferences, form no associations. Because it never saw the real values in the first place.

## Why browser-native?

The obvious alternative would be a server-side proxy: you send your text to our server, we mask it, forward it to the LLM, and restore the response. That model exists — and it has a fundamental flaw.

You've shifted control from one third party (OpenAI) to another third party (the proxy operator). You still have to trust someone — just us now instead of OpenAI.

Browser-native actually solves the problem: detection and mapping run in the same process as your tab. No server knows your data. And it's verifiable — you can open DevTools and confirm that no POST requests with your content are leaving the page.

## What makes Redactly different

Redactly isn't a proxy, a service, or a cloud solution. It's a static web app that runs in your browser.

Three detection tiers let you trade network usage against detection quality: regex for fully offline use, NER for context-aware recognition, WebLLM for maximum accuracy. In all three cases, your text never leaves the browser.

We're building this as an open-source project. Trust through transparency — not through marketing.

